-- title:
--   Function fields are linearly disjoint from constant field extensions
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $F'$ a $K'$-algebra, $K'$ a $K$-algebra and $F'$ both an $F$-algebra and a $K$-algebra, the scalar towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$ being compatible with the given $K$-algebra structure on $F'$; assume $K$ is algebraically closed of characteristic zero. Assume further: there is an $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$; there is an $x \in F'$ transcendental over $K'$ with $F'$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K'(x)$; and the intermediate field of $F'/K'$ generated by the image of $F$ under $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ F\ F'$ is all of $F'$. Then for any index type $\iota$ and any family $b : \iota \to F$ that is linearly independent over $K$, the family of images $i \mapsto \mathrm{algebraMap}\ F\ F'\,(b\,i)$ is linearly independent over $K'$.
--
--   This is the statement that a function field in one variable over an algebraically closed constant field is linearly disjoint from any extension of that constant field, equivalently that $F \otimes_K K' \to F'$ is injective; classically it is the regularity of $F/K$ when $K$ is algebraically closed. It underlies the theory of constant field extensions $F \rightsquigarrow FK'$ used here to compare a curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with its base change, and is cited in the comparison of divisors, Riemann–Roch spaces and regular differentials under such an extension.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_linearIndependent_of_constantFieldExtension.lean

import Mathlib

